Subject: [PATCH] docs: add the ohos-main sync runbook and stack manifest
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

docs/ohos-sync.md is the runbook for rebasing ohos-main onto upstream,
including the fixed decisions (base = upstream tip, lockfile regenerated,
superseded patches kept as annotated empty commits, signoff, AI-assisted
trailer on agent-resolved conflicts, validation gate). docs/ohos-sync/STACK.md
is the ordered patch manifest with a per-sync log; check_stack.py verifies
it against the git log.

The runbook also sets the stack conventions: the fold step may reorder under
the same tree-identity rule, towards docs/CI first, additive as early as
dependencies allow and invasive last; cfg guards shared by several fork
features grow with the stack, each any() arm added by the patch that starts
relying on the code, so every prefix builds with its own features. Its
"After a sync: updating your branches" section covers the routine
pull --rebase path, the cases where it trips, and the deterministic
`git rebase --onto sync/<date> pre-sync/<date> <branch>` form the tags
guarantee.

Vendored third-party code in third_party/<name>, which
third_party/patches/<name>/update.sh generates from upstream plus the
patches next to it, is left out of the patch files, which would otherwise
carry megabytes of generated sources. check_stack.py runs each
update.sh --check instead, which needs network access, as the scripts fetch
upstream.

check_stack.py only ran by hand at sync time, so the `integrity` CI job runs
check_integrity.py on every PR. CI has no `base/<date>` tags, so the script
derives the base as `HEAD~<number of STACK.md entries>`, deepens the depth-1
checkout to it, and runs the manifest and `git am` checks; a PR that adds a
commit without a manifest line shifts the derived base and fails the
subject comparison. The base must also be an ancestor of servo/servo main,
checked via the GitHub compare API. The job checks out the PR head rather
than GitHub's merge commit, which would not be a linear stack.

Validation: uv run docs/ohos-sync/check_stack.py base/<date> passes on ohos-main and reports the manifest, the patch set and each vendored directory as ok. `uv run docs/ohos-sync/check_integrity.py` passes, also in a `git clone --depth 1` with no git identity configured, and fails with a subject diff for an extra commit without a manifest line and with "patch set does not reproduce the head tree" for an edited added line in a patch file. `uv run ruff check docs/ohos-sync` and `uv run ruff format --check docs/ohos-sync` pass.
